Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex regex(R"(^#|\s*(\w+))");
    html = std::regex_replace(regex, R"<h1>$1</h1>");
    html = std::regex_replace(regex, R"<p>$1</p>");
    return html;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. The function uses regular expressions to replace markdown tags with HTML tags, such as `<h1>` and `<p>` tags.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello World\nThis is a paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```html
<h1>Hello World</h1>
<p>This is a paragraph.</p>
```

